50: 【70†tcoratger】 merged 2 commits into\nL151: \nL152: 【71†Plonky3:main】Plonky3/Plonky3:main from\nL153: \nL154: 【72†tcoratger:point-view-equality-tables】tcoratger/Plonky3:point-view-equality-tables Copy head branch name to clipboard\nL155: \nL156: ## Conversation\nL157: \nL158: ###\nL159: \nL160: 【78†Image: @tcoratger†avatars.githubusercontent.com】 【70†tcoratger】 commented 【79†Sep 22, 2026】 •\nL161: \nL162: edited\nL163: \nL164: Loading\nL165: ### Uh oh!\nL166: \nL167: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL168: \nL169: Copy link\nL170: \nL171: Copy Markdown\nL172: ## What this is\nL173: The workspace had six private copies of the same helper: expand a point into its equality table `eq(p, x)` over `{0,1}^n`, in one bit order or the other. They lived in `bus` (three), `multi-stark` and `word-backend` (plus two benches), and were all the naive doubling loop. This PR moves them onto `Point`, where they belong, backs them with one kernel, and deletes every copy.\nL174: \nL175:     point.equality_weights_msb()      // coordinate 0 binds the high bit of the index\nL176:     point.equality_weights_lsb()      // coordinate 0 binds the low bit\nL177:     point.equality_at_vertex(v)       // one entry, no allocation; None if v >= 2^n\nL178: ## A point can now borrow its coordinates\nL179: \nL180: Every call site holds a `&[F]`: a fingerprint, a sumcheck point, a suffix of a product point. Until now `Point` could only own a `Vec`, so using a `Point` method meant copying the slice first.\nL181: \nL182: `Point` now follows the `Poly` / `PolyView` pattern already in the crate:\nL183: \nL184:     pub struct Point<F, S = Vec<F>>(S, PhantomData<F>);\nL185:     pub type PointView<'a, F> = Point<F, &'a [F]>;\nL186: \nL187:     Point::new(fingerprint.as_slice()).equality_weights_lsb()   // no copy\nL188: The default parameter keeps every existing `Point<F>` spelling unchanged. The one inference break across the workspace was `Point::new(iter.collect())` in a single test.\nL189: ## The kernel\nL190: \nL191: Worked example: `p = (a, b)`, LSB order. Start from `[1]`, then let each coordinate double the table in place as a new high bit:\nL192: \nL193:     `[1]  --a-->  [1-a, a]  --b-->  [(1-a)(1-b), a(1-b), (1-a)b, ab]\nL194:                                      x=00        x=01    x=10    x=11\nL195:     `\nL196: \nL197: The MSB order is the same walk with the coordinates entering last-first, so both orders share one body and neither needs a reversed copy.\nL198: \nL199: What makes it fast:\nL200:   * One allocation for the whole `2^n` table, then filled from the front.\nL201:   * Contiguous halves. Each step reads `t` and writes `t * (1 - c)` next to `t * c`, so it streams through `F::Packing` lanes. The old MSB loop interleaved its writes (`2i`, `2i + 1`) and did not vectorize.\nL202:   * One multiplication per entry: `high = low * c`, then `low -= high`.\nL203:   * One parallel region, only where it pays. The index splits as `h | l`, and `eq = eq_high(h) * eq_low(l)`. A tiny serial pass builds the `2^t` seeds, then each task expands its own contiguous chunk. That applies only to unpacked fields (extensions), whose multiplications are compute-bound. A packed base-field step is memory-bound, and splitting it lost 2–5× at `n = 18..20` in measurement, so it stays on one thread. The task size comes from `min_task_len`.\nL204: ## Numbers\nL205: \nL206: Old naive loops vs this PR, 32 threads, `-C target-cpu=native`, `parallel` on. The box was loaded (load average around 15), so treat the multi-threaded cells as indicative.\nL207: field  | n  | old msb  | old lsb  | new msb  | new lsb\nL208: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL209: BabyBear  | 3  | 42 ns  | 45 ns  | 25 ns  | 24 ns\nL210: BabyBear  | 6  | 150 ns  | 137 ns  | 61 ns  | 61 ns\nL211: BabyBear  | 10  | 1.11 µs  | 352 ns  | 159 ns  | 157 ns\nL212: BabyBear  | 14  | 15.0 µs  | 2.31 µs  | 1.40 µs  | 1.44 µs\nL213: BabyBear  | 18  | 262 µs  | 40–69 µs  | 22 µs  | 22 µs\nL214: BabyBear  | 22  | 4.2 ms  | 0.52–0.61 ms  | 0.38–0.44 ms  | 0.38–0.44 ms\nL215: BabyBear⁴  | 3  | 65 ns  | 65 ns  | 52 ns  | 52 ns\nL216: BabyBear⁴  | 10  | 1.65 µs  | 1.62 µs  | 1.34 µs  | 1.34 µs\nL217: BabyBear⁴  | 14  | 23 µs  | 24 µs  | 25 µs  | 27 µs\nL218: BabyBear⁴  | 18  | 428 µs  | 449 µs  | 170–181 µs  | 170–181 µs\nL219: BabyBear⁴  | 22  | 19–30 ms  | 18 ms  | 5.2 ms  | 5.2–5.6 ms\nL220: One cell is a small loss: extension, `n = 14`, where the cost model splits slightly early (25 µs split vs 20 µs serial).\nL221: \nL222: I also tried the crate's existing `Poly::new_from_point` (`eval_eq_batch`) first. On this box it was 1.2–9× slower than the naive loop at `n = 6..14`, because it enters rayon from 9 variables on. That is worth a separate look, since `new_from_point` has other callers.\nL223: ## Call sites\nL224:   * `bus`: fingerprint weights (MSB), memory tuple weights (LSB), product-GKR equality table (LSB) and `eq(point, round_point)`, which now goes through the existing `Point::eval_eq`. The round count is checked by `validate_shape` first, so its length assertion holds. The terminal-share selector uses `equality_at_vertex`.\nL225:   * `multi-stark`: `bus/math.rs` is deleted. The row equality becomes `Point::equality_weights_msb`, the same MSB table as before, and the length-checked `eq` is inlined with its explicit error.\nL226:   * `word-backend`: `shift::transcript::equality_weights` is deleted, and its 20 callers use `Point`.\nL227: ## Tests\nL228: \nL229: In `point.rs`. Every table is pinned against an independent reference, `eq_eval(hypercube(v), p)`:\nL230:   * hand-computed 2-variable tables in both orders, and the empty point;\nL231:   * proptests up to 12 variables, across the kernel's thresholds: MSB against the reference, `lsb[v] = msb[reverse_bits(v)]`, sum of weights `= 1`, a Boolean point giving its indicator, and `equality_at_vertex` equal to the table and `None` outside it;\nL232:   * extension-field tables against the reference;\nL233:   * every chunking of the parallel fill equal to the one-task fill, in both orders. This pins both arms directly instead of relying on the host's pool size.\nL234:   * `equality_at_vertex` with `n >= 64` accepts every `usize`, since each one is a vertex once padded with leading zeros. The previous code returned `None` there.\nL235: ## Breaking\nL236: \nL237: Recorded as `**Breaking:**` lines in the `p3-multilinear-util` and `p3-sumcheck` CHANGELOGs.\nL238: \nL239:   * `Point` gains a store parameter, with a default. `Point::new(iter.collect())` no longer infers; write `Point::<F>::new(iter.collect())`.\nL240:   * `Point::num_variables` is no longer `const`, because `Borrow` is not const.\nL241:   * In `p3-sumcheck`, `BitRingSwitch::num_variables` and `SvoPoint::num_variables_svo` call it and lose `const` too, as do two private helpers.\nL242: 🤖 Generated with 【80†Claude Code†claude.com】\nL243: \nL244: [Input][Input]\nL245: \nL246: Sorry, something went wrong.\nL247: ### Uh oh!\nL248: \nL249: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL250: \nL251: ` 【81†perf(multilinear-util,bus,multi-stark,word-backend)!: borrowed points…】 ` [Button: … ]\nL252: \nL253: [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL254: ` 【81†8e0f9f0】 `\nL255: \nL256:     … and one packed equality-table kernel\nL257: \nL258:     - `Point<F, S = Vec<F>>` is generic over its store, with `PointView<'a, F>`\nL259:       borrowing a slice, mirroring `Poly` / `PolyView`.\nL260:     - `Point::equality_weights_msb`, `equality_weights_lsb` and `equality_at_vertex`\nL261:       replace the six private copies in bus, multi-stark and word-backend.\nL262:     - The tables are built by one kernel: a single allocation, contiguous-halves\nL263:       doubling through `F::Packing`, and one parallel region only for unpacked\nL264:       (extension) fields, sized by `min_task_len`.\nL265: \nL266:     Breaking: `Point` gains a type parameter; `num_variables` / `as_slice` are no\nL267:     longer `const`, and four sumcheck helpers that called them lose `const` too.\nL268: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRelease 0.5.0 (#156) · arkworks-rs/poly-commit@a05ec99 · GitHub (https://github.com/arkworks-rs/poly-commit/commit/a05ec99d0d3e46c8ba0d6d3592980777bc2847e8)\nciteturn11view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.2 years ago; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn10view1\",\"id\":71}); Total lines: 1471\n"
